{
  "dom_id": "step-q31359",
  "name": "Question 3",
  "problem": "Find the slope of $f(x)={x}^{4}\\operatorname{csc}⁡(3x-1)$ at $x=1.$",
  "worked_solution": "To find the slope at a given point, we have to find $\\frac{\\text{d}f}{\\text{d}x}$ at that point.\n\nThis question requires the product rule. Let $u(x)={x}^{4}$ and $v(x)=\\operatorname{csc}⁡(3x-1).$ Then\n\n$$\n{u}^{′}(x)=4{x}^{3},\\,{v}^{′}(x)=-3\\operatorname{csc}⁡(3x-1)\\operatorname{cot}⁡(3x-1).\n$$\n\nNote that we used the chain rule to differentiate $v.$\n\nNow, applying the product rule and simplifying, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}f}{\\text{d}x} & ={u}^{′}(x)\\cdot v(x)+u(x)\\cdot {v}^{′}(x) \\\\  & =(4{x}^{3})(\\operatorname{csc}⁡(3x-1))+({x}^{4})(-3\\operatorname{csc}⁡(3x-1)\\operatorname{cot}⁡(3x-1)) \\\\  & =4{x}^{3}\\operatorname{csc}⁡(3x-1)-3{x}^{4}\\operatorname{csc}⁡(3x-1)\\operatorname{cot}⁡(3x-1) \\\\  & ={x}^{3}\\operatorname{csc}⁡(3x-1)(4-3x\\operatorname{cot}⁡(3x-1)).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the slope at $x=1$ is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{(\\frac{\\text{d}f}{\\text{d}x})|}_{x=1} & =(1)^{3}\\operatorname{csc}⁡(3(1)-1)(4-3(1)\\operatorname{cot}⁡(3(1)-1)) \\\\  & =\\operatorname{csc}⁡2(4-3\\operatorname{cot}⁡2).\\end{aligned}\n$$",
